Fixes that NEST (pinned in scripts/build-nest.sh) needs to compile as static libraries with MinGW-w64 (MSYS2 UCRT64). Applied only by
scripts/build-windows.sh, with `git apply` onto a clean checkout of the pin; the Linux build never sees it. Each hunk is explained here.

libnestutil/config.h.in (one hunk)
  CMake derives NEST_EXITCODE_ABORT / NEST_EXITCODE_SEGFAULT (the "exitcodes" entries of the kernel status) by running a test program through
  cmake/CheckFiles/check_return_val.sh. On Windows that probe cannot see a signal-style exit code and leaves the text "inappropriate file type
  or format", which became `#define NEST_EXITCODE_ABORT inappropriate file type or format` and broke kernel_manager.cpp. On Windows the header
  now carries the shell values Linux reports (128 + SIGABRT, 128 + SIGSEGV). Nothing in the engine reads them.

nestkernel/kernel_manager.cpp (one hunk)
  The exit-code constants are `unsigned int` and are stored in a Dictionary, whose value variant holds `long`. With a 64-bit `long` (Linux)
  unsigned int -> long is a widening the variant's converting assignment accepts; Windows has a 32-bit `long` (LLP64), where it is a narrowing
  and the assignment does not compile. They are `long` constants now (the values are 10 .. 205, so nothing changes).

nestkernel/random_manager.cpp (one hunk)
  `rng_seed < ( 1L << 32 )` is a shift by the full width of a 32-bit long: every seed was refused ("RNG seed must be in (0, 2^32-1)"). `long`
  also cannot hold a seed above 2^31-1, which the Linux build accepts (up to 2^32-1). On Windows only 0 is refused and a seed that arrives as a
  negative long is taken as the unsigned 32-bit value with the same bits (the cast to uint32_t that follows), so the whole range works and is the
  same stream as on Linux.

nestkernel/connection_manager.cpp (one hunk)
  connect-from-arrays checks an integer-valued parameter with `*param > 1L << 31`; with a 32-bit long that is -2^31, so every integer parameter
  (receptor_type, ...) was rejected as "got double". 1LL makes it the intended 2^31.

nestkernel/event_delivery_manager.cpp (one hunk)
  std::max( 2UL, static_cast< size_t >( ... ) ): `unsigned long` is 32 bits on Windows while size_t is 64, so the two arguments differ in type
  and std::max does not deduce. The literal is a size_t now.

nestkernel/mpi_manager.h and mpi_manager.cpp (one hunk each)
  The non-MPI copy overload communicate( vector<unsigned long>&, vector<unsigned long>&, vector<int>& ) is what SPManager reaches with its
  std::vector< size_t >, which is `unsigned long` only where long is 64 bits. On Windows an overload for `unsigned long long` is added (same body).

nestkernel/mpi_manager.h (two hunks)
  gethostname() is a Winsock function on Windows (declared in <winsock2.h>, and it fails until WSAStartup ran), so the non-MPI
  MPIManager::get_processor_name() did not compile. It only fills the "processor name" string of the kernel status, so on Windows it asks
  for the computer name with GetComputerNameA (kernel32), declared by hand: <windows.h> would otherwise be pulled into every NEST file
  through this header and its macros (min, max, ERROR, ...) collide with NEST's names.

Not a patch, but part of the same port (scripts/build-windows.sh, engine/CMakeLists.txt): MinGW lays out bit-fields like MSVC by default, which breaks
NEST's compile-time size checks (SpikeData == 8 bytes, TargetData == 12); everything that includes NEST headers is built with -mno-ms-bitfields.

